1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
What if the windows in your house came with a tax bill?

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
In 1696, England introduced a tax linked to the number of windows in a home.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
More windows could mean a larger bill. Assessors counted the openings.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
The number went into the household's assessment.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Some owners responded by closing windows to reduce what they owed.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Surviving tax records even note openings that had been closed.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
But a bricked-up window doesn't always prove tax avoidance.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Some Georgian houses used blank windows to keep their facades symmetrical.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Historians have to distinguish design choices from tax responses.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The tax remained for more than 150 years, until it was repealed in 1851.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Critics had raised concerns about the effects of dim, poorly ventilated homes.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
A rule meant to count windows changed how people thought about their walls.
